More data:  I have not, in fact, researched whether any Usenet libel
suits preceded Rindos vs. Hardwick, but other people do seem to be
confident that it's the first.  I've completely lost track of whether
there have been more since then, but there's at least a second one,
now:

	... British physicist Laurence Godfrey
	is suing fellow physicist Philip Halam-
	Baker for posting negative messages about
	him ...  "These messages went into news-
	groups and were read by my colleagues and
	students," Godfrey charges.

	Godfrey has filed suit in England, where
	libel laws are particulary harsh.  . . .
	[W]ould network operators also be liable?
	Godfrey thinks so; he's pursuing the Can-
	adian universities that run the newsgroups
	in question.

	. . .

I extracted this, without permission, from a byline-less article
"Libel On The Net" which appears on page 10 of *INFORMATIONWEEK*
for 31 October 1994.  I have no idea what the reporter means by
"... Canadian universities that run the newsgroups ..."

I offer a disclaimer:  I have exchanged correspondence with at
least three of the four plaintiffs and defendants I mention above.
Essentially all the content of our transactions was professional
(RISC design, URLs-by-email, ...) and unrelated to litigation.
Even if we had been discussing the suits, I can't imagine how that
would affect what I write; however, in case some reader does find
it germane, I make it explicit that I have a tenuous personal con-
nection to the actors in these affairs.
