Kevin Buhr <buhr@stat.wisc.edu> wrote:
>dxb37@po.CWRU.Edu (Dean Boland) writes:
>| 
>| What is with the Post-office declining to print a stamp which
>| pictures the mushroom cloud of the atomic bomb over Hiroshima
>| and proclaims the victory that the bomb decisively won.
>[...]
>It seems to me that depicting the dropping of the atom bomb on
>Hiroshima on a postage stamp is simply unnecessarily tasteless.  

It seems something of a Catch-22 to me.  World War II was arguably
the definitive period of this century; I see nothing improper in
commemorating our victory in that conflict.  It could be argued
that the first use of atomic weapons was the defining moment of 
that conflict (and the era that followed).  Since this series of 
stamps is commemorating the significant events of America's parti-
cipation in World War II, how can we *not* mention Hiroshima/Nagasaki?  

It it possible that our obligation to history should outweigh our
feelings?  Before you answer, consider:
	- the various attempts to change history textbooks in the USA. 
	- the groups which assert that today's white Americans should be 
	  held responsible for the slavery of centuries past; you certainly 
	  don't hear anyone standing up for 'not offending' in *that* matter, 
	  do you? 
	- the removal of literature from our libraries because of 'offensive'
	  language.  (_Tom Sawyer_, written decades ago, is under attack for
	  its use of the word 'nigger')
In our discussions of academic freedom and censorship, many have spoken
against the actions listed above; why should our position on this postage
stamp controversy differ?

Perhaps we need a stamp with the caption "Atomic Bombing of Hiroshima"
with an illustration of an American clearing their throat in embarrass-
ment, eh? 

We have an obligation to both our history and our children.  Part of that
obligation is the straightforward presentation of our history to our chil-
dren.  Sanitizing our past in the name of 'taste' or 'inoffensiveness' is
a disservice to the very thing we are attempting to commemorate.

--Wes

ps> As an aside, I find the 'outrage' rather ironic in light of the Japa-
    nese government's *consistent* refusal to issue any sort of real apo-
    logy (or acceptance of blame) for their actions in World War II.  (It
    is possible that this is changing; the news media has reported several
    comments from Japanese officials that skirt the unspoken ban on admit-
    ting guilt/responsibility, and a few Japanese history textbooks are
    now mentioning Japan's actions in a less than neutral fashion.)


